# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Agent Execution Budget Policy

Budgets detect thrashing, scope growth and failing strategies. They are maxima, not targets, and never justify
weakening validation, security, testing or acceptance criteria. Detail:
[budgets reference](../.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/budgets.md).

## Ownership

{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} assigns the budget class in each Goal (default {{DEFAULT_BUDGET}}). {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}}
works within it, may recommend a change during planning and never increases its own budget. A change after Plan
approval needs {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} approval recorded in the Goal or Plan history, and never implies scope,
architecture or acceptance changes.

## Goal execution attempts

- One attempt is one Goal-level try. Commands, test batches, inner fix loops, rebases, handoffs, checkpoints and
  restarts do not consume attempts.
- The initial implementation is attempt 1. An attempt ends at a review-ready Result, a report that it cannot
  satisfy the Goal, or an abandoned strategy. A retry or an authorized correction round consumes a new attempt.
- Workers and subagents share the Goal's allowance; there is one ledger, kept by the implementer.

## Classes and checkpoints

| Class | Maximum | Checkpoint |
|---|---|---|
| B3 | 3 | after attempt 2 |
| B5 | 5 | after attempt 3 |
| B7 | 7 | after attempt 4 |
| B15 (exceptional) | 15 | after attempts 8 and 12 |

A checkpoint reassesses criteria done and remaining, failures, repeated patterns, uncertainty, convergence and
bounds. It is not an automatic stop.

## Exhaustion and extensions

On exhaustion: stop, preserve state, keep failing criteria, update the Result and return control. An extension
records the previous and new budget, attempts used (preserved), reason, remaining work and the approving supervisor.
Counters are never reset.

## Effort

Recommendations only: planning {{PLANNING_EFFORT}}, implementation {{IMPLEMENTATION_EFFORT}}. Record unverifiable
runtime effort as unknown.

## Workload-based model selection

Model choice is independent of effort and budget. With the owner's provider/model
selection, prefer a stronger reasoning model for complex architecture/security/data
planning, difficult test design and diagnosis, or repeated unresolved corrections.
Use the ordinary coding model for routine planning/implementation, precisely
specified fixes, test execution/reporting and coordination. For Claude, the
owner-approved examples are Fable 5.1 for the former and Opus 5.5 for the latter;
verify current availability before adopting these examples in another project.

Apply the configured role effort separately. State model/effort before a new phase,
honor owner choices and verify actual configuration where supported, otherwise
record unknown. Never assume an instruction changed a worker's runtime. Do not
interrupt an active batch, redo completed work, reset budgets or weaken gates
solely to change models. A stronger model does not accelerate the underlying
Docker/build/test process. These recommendations add no provider dependency or
automatic configuration change.

## Result accounting

Every Result has an Execution Budget section: class, maximum, attempts used, and an attempt log with each
attempt's contributions, outcome and boundary reason. Individual failures and commands are validation history, not
attempts. Acceptance remains {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}}'s decision.
